# 1bit-console - what /etc/inittab respawns on tty1.
#
# Puppy's inittab says `tty1::respawn:plogin`, and plogin does a lot before its last line: it reads
# /etc/plogin, sources /etc/rc.d/PUPSTATE, lays out /root and a user's home, rewrites invalid symlinks, then
# `exec getty -n -l /bin/autologin 115200,38400,9600 $TTY`. On the 2026-11-02 board boot (cycle 13, headless
# entry) that chain left **no prompt on tty1** - measured over ssh: `getty 38400 tty2` running and nothing on
# tty1 - while the same image gave a prompt on tty1 in QEMU. A console-only machine whose console has nothing
# to log into is not headless, it is unreachable, so the appliance's own console cannot depend on that chain.
#
# On a headless boot (pfix=nox) this is the appliance's own CONSOLE MENU: the owner's words on the cycle-14
# board boot were "no menu system", and a bare root prompt is not an appliance - a person in front of the box
# needs to see Lemonade/GPU/NPU/Secure Boot/fans/data and to reach the six things the box can do without a
# browser. 1bit-console-menu owns that surface and item 7 is bash, so nothing is lost by replacing the prompt.
# Every other boot still runs plogin, because it is what lays out the first X session on the desktop entry.
# The "Install 1bit OS to a disk" entry (owner, cycle 14): tty1 IS the installer there. It is done here
# rather than by a service of its own so nothing else competes for the same terminal - a getty and the
# installer both reading tty1 would eat each other's keystrokes.
if grep -q '1bit.installer=1' /proc/cmdline 2> /dev/null; then
    exec /usr/local/bin/1bit-console-install
fi
if ! grep -q 'pfix=[^ ]*nox' /proc/cmdline 2> /dev/null; then
    exec /bin/plogin
fi
# Cycle 26, item 1: nothing else may write to tty1 while the console owns it. The boot line has no `loglevel=`,
# so console_loglevel is 7 - every kernel message prints to tty0, and tty0 IS this screen. Measured on the c25 take:
# the 04:52 NPU TDR printed 15 err + 5 warn lines (aie2_legacy_tdr_detect, then ~18 `*ERROR* aie2_set_cmd_timeout:`
# firmware-report lines through 08:52:09Z) straight over the TUI. Quieter *console* does not lose the evidence: the
# ring is 32 MB (log_buf_len=32M) and /data/logs/dmesg.log copies it, so `dmesg` and 1bit-proof keep every line.
# Set before the TUI starts, not in the boot line, because the kernel's own boot transcript on both consoles is
# what g2-probe reads; this only silences printk once a person-facing screen is up.
#
# This alone was NOT enough, and the gate caught it (`U2 console quiet` came back `console-loglevel=4`): Ubuntu's base
# ships /usr/lib/sysctl.d/55-console-messages.conf with `kernel.printk = 4 4 1 7`, Puppy's rc.sysinit calls
# `sysctl --system` late in boot, and it landed AFTER this line - so the level was 7 at t=44 s and 4 from t=50 s on,
# which still prints KERN_ERR, i.e. exactly the TDR lines this item exists for. The fix is the drop-in beside this
# script (embed/etc/sysctl.d/60-1bit-console.conf, measured to win: `sysctl --system` then ends `1 4 1 7`). Both are
# kept: the drop-in wins whenever rc.sysinit runs, and this line covers the other order and re-asserts afterwards.
dmesg -n 1 2> /dev/null || true
# Cycle 18 (pi-tui, ~/briefs/TCONSOLE-TUI.md): the full-screen TUI is the console when the image has one. It does
# not exit on `q` on purpose (a console that drops to a bare prompt is the cycle-13 defect), so BOTH a zero exit and
# a non-zero one mean the same thing here: something went wrong or a person quit the screen, and the dialog menu is
# the fallback the cycle-15 gates were written against. Nothing to fall back from if the binary is absent.
if [ -x /usr/local/bin/1bit-console-tui ]; then
    /usr/local/bin/1bit-console-tui
    # WHY it left, kept: the TUI prints its own reason to stderr (a terminal it cannot drive, raw mode refused) and
    # this line carries the status, on the screen for the person in front of the box AND in /data/logs for the one
    # reading afterwards. Both halves matter: a console that falls back silently is a defect nobody can name later.
    rc=$?
    printf '\n1bit-console-tui exited (%s) - starting the dialog menu\n' "$rc" > /dev/tty1 2> /dev/null || true
    printf '1bit-console-tui exited (%s) at %s\n' "$rc" "$(date -Is)" \
        >> /data/logs/console-tui.log 2> /dev/null || true
fi
exec /usr/local/bin/1bit-console-menu
